window.__courseChapter("v2-13", {"v2-13-00-overview": {"title": "Overview: the big ideas", "summary": "The chapter's key concepts and how its sections fit together.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-13", "sections": ["Purpose", "concept map"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "Welcome to **Security & Privacy** (Vol II). This overview gives you the chapter's big ideas before the section-by-section lessons. Read the claims once, flip the flashcards, then take the warm-up quiz: it is fine to miss questions here, the lessons that follow fill the gaps."}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Security & Privacy, Purpose", "md": "Many useful machine learning systems depend on personal data, institutional knowledge, or behavioral patterns, creating tension between utility and protection. Models can encode sensitive information and later reveal it through their outputs, extending risk beyond transient data processing. An authorized request can expose a private training example, while accepted training data can be manipulated to alter future behavior. In both cases, the software may execute as designed while the service violates its intended boundaries."}, {"type": "flow", "title": "The path through this chapter", "nodes": [{"label": "The Expanded Attack Surface", "detail": "Lesson 1 covers this section."}, {"label": "Security and Privacy Foundations", "detail": "Lesson 2 covers this section."}, {"label": "Learning from Security Breaches", "detail": "Lesson 3 covers this section."}, {"label": "Systematic Threat Analysis and Risk Asse", "detail": "Lesson 4 covers this section."}, {"label": "Model-Specific Attack Vectors", "detail": "Lesson 5 covers this section."}, {"label": "Fleet-Internal Trust Boundaries", "detail": "Lesson 6 covers this section."}, {"label": "Hardware-Level Security Vulnerabilities", "detail": "Lesson 7 covers this section."}]}, {"type": "table", "headers": ["Concept", "The claim to remember"], "rows": [["**ML threat model**", "Attacks on a learned system arrive through interfaces the fleet exposes on purpose, so a threat model writes down asset, trust boundary, adversary capability and goal, and control, and keeps model visibility separate from write capability; the serving interface adds prompt injection, which crosses the boundary between instructions and data, and denial of wallet, which spends the cost budget while the endpoint stays available."], ["**Extraction budgets**", "Exact artifact theft and approximate behavioral theft need different controls, and because a daily rate limit slows collection without capping its lifetime total, query-based extraction is bounded by cumulative per-principal budgets and the output contract."], ["**Poisoning as a bilevel attack**", "A poisoner needs write access to something the model learns from and solves a bilevel problem over the defender's training, so targeted, backdoor, and subpopulation attacks can leave aggregate validation accuracy unchanged, and clean-label poisons that keep every label correct pass any label audit, as concept poisoning of a text-to-image model (Nightshade) shows at a dose of a few hundred images."], ["**Breakdown point of the gradient mean**", "In synchronous data parallelism one compromised worker contributes to every replica's update and the mean's breakdown point is zero, so integrity needs robust aggregation rules whose gather-based traffic exceeds a ring AllReduce's."], ["**Security controls priced on a C3 axis**", "Every isolation and confidentiality control spends capacity in a specific Fleet Law term, so encrypting bandwidth-bound collectives slows T_comm and partitioning, scrubbing, and tenant-scoped caches strand capacity or forfeit hits."], ["**Provenance bounds trust**", "A model can be trusted only as far as a verifiable record of its data, code, weights, and hardware extends, so signatures establish origin but not benign content, and the release record must bind every input the artifact depends on."], ["**Leakage through authorized outputs**", "Persistent learning means a correctly executing model can leak its training data through authorized queries (membership in one bit, properties by inversion, verbatim records by memorization), so storage access control cannot protect it, an empirical membership-inference audit is only a lower bound on that leakage, and differential privacy supplies the upper bound."], ["**Differential privacy**", "(epsilon, delta)-differential privacy bounds how much one protected unit can change a release, named by an adjacency relation, so it holds against any auxiliary information, and noise scaled to sensitivity over epsilon costs small groups the most."], ["**Hardware trust questions**", "Hardware security is four narrow deployment decisions (processor isolation, physical access and fault injection, side-channel observability, provenance and lifecycle trust), each answered by one primitive, and fleet procurement correlates their failure."], ["**Privacy techniques by excluded observer**", "De-identification and k-anonymity constrain only the shape of a released table, and federated learning, secure aggregation, homomorphic encryption, secure computation, and synthetic data each exclude a different observer, and none of them bounds what the released model or aggregate reveals."], ["**Privacy budget account**", "Privacy loss accumulates across every data-dependent release about the same population, so DP-SGD's many sampled steps need a tested accountant whose recorded inputs (sampling, noise, steps, clipping, adjacency) form the release record, and the cumulative budget becomes a release gate."]]}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "key", "title": "Formulas you will meet", "md": "$$\\text{Dec}(sk, \\text{Eval}(pk, f, \\text{Enc}(pk, x))) = f(x)$$\n\n$$\\max_{\\mathcal{S}_{\\text{poison}}} \\ \\mathcal{L}(f_{\\mathcal{S} \\cup \\mathcal{S}_{\\text{poison}}}, \\mathcal{S}_{\\text{test}})$$\n\n$$\\min_{\\mathcal{S}_{\\text{poison}}} \\ \\mathcal{L}(f_{\\mathcal{S} \\cup \\mathcal{S}_{\\text{poison}}}, x_t, y_t)$$\n\n$$\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{KL}}(p_{\\text{user}} \\lVert p_{\\text{expected}}) > \\tau_{\\text{alert}}$$"}, {"type": "flashcards", "id": "v2-13-cards-concepts", "title": "Key concepts", "cards": [{"front": "ML threat model", "back": "Attacks on a learned system arrive through interfaces the fleet exposes on purpose, so a threat model writes down asset, trust boundary, adversary capability and goal, and control, and keeps model visibility separate from write capability; the serving interface adds prompt injection, which crosses the boundary between instructions and data, and denial of wallet, which spends the cost budget while the endpoint stays available."}, {"front": "Extraction budgets", "back": "Exact artifact theft and approximate behavioral theft need different controls, and because a daily rate limit slows collection without capping its lifetime total, query-based extraction is bounded by cumulative per-principal budgets and the output contract."}, {"front": "Poisoning as a bilevel attack", "back": "A poisoner needs write access to something the model learns from and solves a bilevel problem over the defender's training, so targeted, backdoor, and subpopulation attacks can leave aggregate validation accuracy unchanged, and clean-label poisons that keep every label correct pass any label audit, as concept poisoning of a text-to-image model (Nightshade) shows at a dose of a few hundred images."}, {"front": "Breakdown point of the gradient mean", "back": "In synchronous data parallelism one compromised worker contributes to every replica's update and the mean's breakdown point is zero, so integrity needs robust aggregation rules whose gather-based traffic exceeds a ring AllReduce's."}, {"front": "Security controls priced on a C3 axis", "back": "Every isolation and confidentiality control spends capacity in a specific Fleet Law term, so encrypting bandwidth-bound collectives slows T_comm and partitioning, scrubbing, and tenant-scoped caches strand capacity or forfeit hits."}, {"front": "Provenance bounds trust", "back": "A model can be trusted only as far as a verifiable record of its data, code, weights, and hardware extends, so signatures establish origin but not benign content, and the release record must bind every input the artifact depends on."}, {"front": "Leakage through authorized outputs", "back": "Persistent learning means a correctly executing model can leak its training data through authorized queries (membership in one bit, properties by inversion, verbatim records by memorization), so storage access control cannot protect it, an empirical membership-inference audit is only a lower bound on that leakage, and differential privacy supplies the upper bound."}, {"front": "Differential privacy", "back": "(epsilon, delta)-differential privacy bounds how much one protected unit can change a release, named by an adjacency relation, so it holds against any auxiliary information, and noise scaled to sensitivity over epsilon costs small groups the most."}, {"front": "Hardware trust questions", "back": "Hardware security is four narrow deployment decisions (processor isolation, physical access and fault injection, side-channel observability, provenance and lifecycle trust), each answered by one primitive, and fleet procurement correlates their failure."}, {"front": "Privacy techniques by excluded observer", "back": "De-identification and k-anonymity constrain only the shape of a released table, and federated learning, secure aggregation, homomorphic encryption, secure computation, and synthetic data each exclude a different observer, and none of them bounds what the released model or aggregate reveals."}]}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-13-quiz-warmup", "title": "Warm-up: match the big ideas", "questions": [{"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **ML threat model**?", "options": ["Hardware security is four narrow deployment decisions (processor isolation, physical access and fault injection, side-channel observability, provenance and lifecycle trust), each answered by one primitive, and fleet procurement correlates their failure.", "In synchronous data parallelism one compromised worker contributes to every replica's update and the mean's breakdown point is zero, so integrity needs robust aggregation rules whose gather-based traffic exceeds a ring AllReduce's.", "Attacks on a learned system arrive through interfaces the fleet exposes on purpose, so a threat model writes down asset, trust boundary, adversary capability and goal, and control, and keeps model visibility separate from write capability; the serving interface adds prompt injection, which crosses the boundary between instructions and data, and denial of wallet, which spends the cost budget while the endpoint stays available.", "(epsilon, delta)-differential privacy bounds how much one protected unit can change a release, named by an adjacency relation, so it holds against any auxiliary information, and noise scaled to sensitivity over epsilon costs small groups the most."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "**ML threat model:** Attacks on a learned system arrive through interfaces the fleet exposes on purpose, so a threat model writes down asset, trust boundary, adversary capability and goal, and control, and keeps model visibility separate from write capability; the serving interface adds prompt injection, which crosses the boundary between instructions and data, and denial of wallet, which spends the cost budget while the endpoint stays available.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is ML threat model itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Extraction budgets**?", "options": ["Privacy loss accumulates across every data-dependent release about the same population, so DP-SGD's many sampled steps need a tested accountant whose recorded inputs (sampling, noise, steps, clipping, adjacency) form the release record, and the cumulative budget becomes a release gate.", "Hardware security is four narrow deployment decisions (processor isolation, physical access and fault injection, side-channel observability, provenance and lifecycle trust), each answered by one primitive, and fleet procurement correlates their failure.", "Persistent learning means a correctly executing model can leak its training data through authorized queries (membership in one bit, properties by inversion, verbatim records by memorization), so storage access control cannot protect it, an empirical membership-inference audit is only a lower bound on that leakage, and differential privacy supplies the upper bound.", "Exact artifact theft and approximate behavioral theft need different controls, and because a daily rate limit slows collection without capping its lifetime total, query-based extraction is bounded by cumulative per-principal budgets and the output contract."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Extraction budgets:** Exact artifact theft and approximate behavioral theft need different controls, and because a daily rate limit slows collection without capping its lifetime total, query-based extraction is bounded by cumulative per-principal budgets and the output contract.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Extraction budgets itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Poisoning as a bilevel attack**?", "options": ["De-identification and k-anonymity constrain only the shape of a released table, and federated learning, secure aggregation, homomorphic encryption, secure computation, and synthetic data each exclude a different observer, and none of them bounds what the released model or aggregate reveals.", "A poisoner needs write access to something the model learns from and solves a bilevel problem over the defender's training, so targeted, backdoor, and subpopulation attacks can leave aggregate validation accuracy unchanged, and clean-label poisons that keep every label correct pass any label audit, as concept poisoning of a text-to-image model (Nightshade) shows at a dose of a few hundred images.", "In synchronous data parallelism one compromised worker contributes to every replica's update and the mean's breakdown point is zero, so integrity needs robust aggregation rules whose gather-based traffic exceeds a ring AllReduce's.", "A model can be trusted only as far as a verifiable record of its data, code, weights, and hardware extends, so signatures establish origin but not benign content, and the release record must bind every input the artifact depends on."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Poisoning as a bilevel attack:** A poisoner needs write access to something the model learns from and solves a bilevel problem over the defender's training, so targeted, backdoor, and subpopulation attacks can leave aggregate validation accuracy unchanged, and clean-label poisons that keep every label correct pass any label audit, as concept poisoning of a text-to-image model (Nightshade) shows at a dose of a few hundred images.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Poisoning as a bilevel attack itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Breakdown point of the gradient mean**?", "options": ["A poisoner needs write access to something the model learns from and solves a bilevel problem over the defender's training, so targeted, backdoor, and subpopulation attacks can leave aggregate validation accuracy unchanged, and clean-label poisons that keep every label correct pass any label audit, as concept poisoning of a text-to-image model (Nightshade) shows at a dose of a few hundred images.", "De-identification and k-anonymity constrain only the shape of a released table, and federated learning, secure aggregation, homomorphic encryption, secure computation, and synthetic data each exclude a different observer, and none of them bounds what the released model or aggregate reveals.", "Attacks on a learned system arrive through interfaces the fleet exposes on purpose, so a threat model writes down asset, trust boundary, adversary capability and goal, and control, and keeps model visibility separate from write capability; the serving interface adds prompt injection, which crosses the boundary between instructions and data, and denial of wallet, which spends the cost budget while the endpoint stays available.", "In synchronous data parallelism one compromised worker contributes to every replica's update and the mean's breakdown point is zero, so integrity needs robust aggregation rules whose gather-based traffic exceeds a ring AllReduce's."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Breakdown point of the gradient mean:** In synchronous data parallelism one compromised worker contributes to every replica's update and the mean's breakdown point is zero, so integrity needs robust aggregation rules whose gather-based traffic exceeds a ring AllReduce's.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Breakdown point of the gradient mean itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Security controls priced on a C3 axis**?", "options": ["Attacks on a learned system arrive through interfaces the fleet exposes on purpose, so a threat model writes down asset, trust boundary, adversary capability and goal, and control, and keeps model visibility separate from write capability; the serving interface adds prompt injection, which crosses the boundary between instructions and data, and denial of wallet, which spends the cost budget while the endpoint stays available.", "De-identification and k-anonymity constrain only the shape of a released table, and federated learning, secure aggregation, homomorphic encryption, secure computation, and synthetic data each exclude a different observer, and none of them bounds what the released model or aggregate reveals.", "Exact artifact theft and approximate behavioral theft need different controls, and because a daily rate limit slows collection without capping its lifetime total, query-based extraction is bounded by cumulative per-principal budgets and the output contract.", "Every isolation and confidentiality control spends capacity in a specific Fleet Law term, so encrypting bandwidth-bound collectives slows T_comm and partitioning, scrubbing, and tenant-scoped caches strand capacity or forfeit hits."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Security controls priced on a C3 axis:** Every isolation and confidentiality control spends capacity in a specific Fleet Law term, so encrypting bandwidth-bound collectives slows T_comm and partitioning, scrubbing, and tenant-scoped caches strand capacity or forfeit hits.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Security controls priced on a C3 axis itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["ML threat model: Attacks on a learned system arrive through interfaces the fleet exposes on purpose, so a threat model writes down asset, trust boundary, adversary capability and goal, and control, and keeps model visibility separate from write capability; the serving interface adds prompt injection, which crosses the boundary between instructions and data, and denial of wallet, which spends the cost budget while the endpoint stays available.", "Extraction budgets: Exact artifact theft and approximate behavioral theft need different controls, and because a daily rate limit slows collection without capping its lifetime total, query-based extraction is bounded by cumulative per-principal budgets and the output contract.", "Poisoning as a bilevel attack: A poisoner needs write access to something the model learns from and solves a bilevel problem over the defender's training, so targeted, backdoor, and subpopulation attacks can leave aggregate validation accuracy unchanged, and clean-label poisons that keep every label correct pass any label audit, as concept poisoning of a text-to-image model (Nightshade) shows at a dose of a few hundred images.", "Breakdown point of the gradient mean: In synchronous data parallelism one compromised worker contributes to every replica's update and the mean's breakdown point is zero, so integrity needs robust aggregation rules whose gather-based traffic exceeds a ring AllReduce's."]}], "terms": [], "id": "v2-13-00-overview", "chapter": "v2-13", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 10}, "v2-13-01-the-expanded-attack-surface": {"title": "The Expanded Attack Surface", "summary": "Analyze why traditional network perimeter defenses fail to prevent algorithmic attacks on ML models.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-13", "sections": ["The Expanded Attack Surface"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **The Expanded Attack Surface** in *Security & Privacy* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze why traditional network perimeter defenses fail to prevent algorithmic attacks on ML models\n- Compare the threat models and defense objectives of ML security versus ML privacy\n- Distinguish access and storage controls from privacy exposure through authorized model outputs"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Security & Privacy, § The Expanded Attack Surface", "md": "A serving fleet can meet its latency target while an authenticated client collects enough responses to copy model behavior. A federated training job can converge while a participating device submits poisoned updates. The release gates and hierarchical monitoring that govern the fleet can report both systems healthy, because a copied model and a planted backdoor need not move any availability, latency, or aggregate-quality signal. Operational health alone therefore cannot establish security."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "Pay attention to: Algorithmic attack surface vs traditional perimeter security; Persistent learning vs transient processing; Security vs privacy definitions and threat models; Complementary access controls and release-level privacy."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: An inference service encrypts its stored training records and authenticates every caller, yet an authorized caller extracts memorized records from its predictions. Explain what the two controls protect and which privacy question remains.", "md": "Encryption protects stored records from observers without the key, while authentication establishes the caller's identity. Authorization separately determines permitted actions. Neither alone bounds what an authorized prediction reveals about the training data. The remaining question is what the released model discloses about a protected contribution. That requires a release-level privacy analysis rather than assuming that secure storage and authenticated access establish privacy."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-13-01-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A security team deploys an enterprise ML inference service behind a web application firewall with strict mutual TLS (mTLS) authentication and rate limiting. Despite zero network-level intrusions, an external adversary reconstructs the model's proprietary decision boundaries and extracts memorized training records. What fundamental characteristic of ML systems explains this vulnerability?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Traditional IT security protects the infrastructure perimeter through access control, transport encryption, and firewalls. However, ML systems introduce an orthogonal algorithmic attack surface: properly authenticated API queries containing crafted inputs leverage the model's learned parameters directly to elicit memorized data or reconstruct decision boundaries. The assertion regarding unencrypted tensors confuses transport-layer security with model-layer information leakage. The claim about JSON payload inspection confuses firewall protocol parsing with semantic model exploitation. The assertion regarding rate limiting confuses stream accounting with algorithmic leakage.", "type": "single", "options": ["ML models act on and compress learned data, exposing an algorithmic attack surface through properly authenticated inputs and valid response signals.", "mTLS certificates fail to encrypt floating-point tensors at the network transport layer.", "Traditional firewalls cannot inspect JSON payloads formatted as numerical arrays.", "Token-bucket rate limiting in standard firewalls is incapable of tracking HTTP/2 request streams."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze why traditional network perimeter defenses fail to prevent algorithmic attacks on ML models."]}, {"q": "An ML platform engineer must classify two distinct failure modes: (1) a malicious actor submits crafted audio noise to cause an emergency dispatch classifier to miss distress calls, and (2) an observer examines a public salary prediction model and infers whether a specific executive was included in the training set. How should these two issues be categorized according to threat model and objective?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Failure (1) represents a security attack where an active adversary intentionally crafts inputs to compromise system integrity (evasion/misclassification). Failure (2) represents a privacy issue where an observer infers sensitive individual participation from model outputs even without attacking system availability or exploiting software vulnerabilities. Classifying the audio perturbation as a privacy breach confuses confidentiality with operational integrity. Treating both as network infrastructure incidents ignores the algorithmic nature of the attacks. Classifying evasion as stochastic robustness overlooks the intentional worst-case adversary.", "type": "single", "options": ["(1) is a privacy violation targeting confidentiality; (2) is a security breach targeting system availability.", "(1) is an adversarial security attack targeting model integrity; (2) is a privacy leakage issue targeting participation inference.", "Both (1) and (2) are pure infrastructure security incidents requiring network-layer firewall rule updates.", "(1) is a stochastic robustness failure; (2) is an unauthorized access control violation requiring credential rotation."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: compare the threat models and defense objectives of ML security versus ML privacy."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze why traditional network perimeter defenses fail to prevent algorithmic attacks on ML models.", "You can now compare the threat models and defense objectives of ML security versus ML privacy.", "You can now distinguish access and storage controls from privacy exposure through authorized model outputs."]}], "id": "v2-13-01-the-expanded-attack-surface", "chapter": "v2-13", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-13-02-security-and-privacy-foundations": {"title": "Security and Privacy Foundations", "summary": "Work through Security and Privacy Foundations.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-13", "sections": ["Security and Privacy Foundations", "Security defined", "Privacy defined", "Security versus privacy"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Security and Privacy Foundations** in *Security & Privacy* (Vol II)."}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Security & Privacy, § Security and Privacy Foundations", "md": "Defense starts by separating two concerns that often share the same vocabulary. Security asks how an adversary can steal, manipulate, or disable the fleet; privacy asks how sensitive information can leak or be inferred even when the system behaves as designed. Keeping those questions separate determines which controls belong at each layer: authentication and isolation around adversarial pathways, provenance and monitoring across the release chain, and privacy accounting wherever learned parameters carry traces of the data that produced them."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Security defined\n- Privacy defined\n- Security versus privacy"}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: In two or three sentences, explain why Differential privacy matters for the system this section describes.", "md": "Compare your answer with the book's claim: (epsilon, delta)-differential privacy bounds how much one protected unit can change a release, named by an adjacency relation, so it holds against any auxiliary information, and noise scaled to sensitivity over epsilon costs small groups the most."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-13-02-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"type": "single", "q": "The section **Security and Privacy Foundations** develops one of the chapter's claims. Which one?", "options": ["(epsilon, delta)-differential privacy bounds how much one protected unit can change a release, named by an adjacency relation, so it holds against any auxiliary information, and noise scaled to sensitivity over epsilon costs small groups the most.", "In synchronous data parallelism one compromised worker contributes to every replica's update and the mean's breakdown point is zero, so integrity needs robust aggregation rules whose gather-based traffic exceeds a ring AllReduce's.", "De-identification and k-anonymity constrain only the shape of a released table, and federated learning, secure aggregation, homomorphic encryption, secure computation, and synthetic data each exclude a different observer, and none of them bounds what the released model or aggregate reveals.", "Attacks on a learned system arrive through interfaces the fleet exposes on purpose, so a threat model writes down asset, trust boundary, adversary capability and goal, and control, and keeps model visibility separate from write capability; the serving interface adds prompt injection, which crosses the boundary between instructions and data, and denial of wallet, which spends the cost budget while the endpoint stays available."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "This section develops **Differential privacy**: (epsilon, delta)-differential privacy bounds how much one protected unit can change a release, named by an adjacency relation, so it holds against any auxiliary information, and noise scaled to sensitivity over epsilon costs small groups the most.", "hints": ["Re-read the excerpt and the section map: which idea is this section building toward?"]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You have worked through Security and Privacy Foundations."]}], "id": "v2-13-02-security-and-privacy-foundations", "chapter": "v2-13", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-13-03-learning-from-security-breaches": {"title": "Learning from Security Breaches", "summary": "Analyze why simple aggregation fails to protect privacy in high-dimensional behavioral datasets.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-13", "sections": ["Learning from Security Breaches", "Stuxnet and supply chain compromise", "Insufficient isolation in the Jeep Cherokee hack", "Weaponized endpoints and the Mirai botnet"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Learning from Security Breaches** in *Security & Privacy* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze why simple aggregation fails to protect privacy in high-dimensional behavioral datasets\n- Compare the attack mechanism of the Stuxnet worm with model backdoor attacks in ML pipelines\n- Evaluate the isolation architecture required between external-facing ML inference APIs and physical actuators\n- Analyze the dual threat posed by compromised edge devices in federated ML architectures\n- Explain the vulnerability window represented by zero-day exploits in ML serving infrastructure"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Security & Privacy, § Learning from Security Breaches", "md": "A public heatmap built for runners became a military-intelligence leak when sparse GPS traces outlined movement around sensitive sites. That privacy failure belongs beside three landmark security breaches because each case turns an abstract control objective into an operational constraint. Behavioral telemetry can identify people. Supply-chain compromise can alter physical systems. Weak isolation can expose safety-critical control paths. Default credentials can turn cheap devices into attack infrastructure."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Stuxnet and supply chain compromise\n- Insufficient isolation in the Jeep Cherokee hack\n- Weaponized endpoints and the Mirai botnet\n\nPay attention to: Strava heatmap aggregate privacy leakage; Stuxnet supply chain and silent telemetry manipulation analog; Jeep Cherokee isolation failure between interfaces and actuators; Mirai botnet weaponized endpoints and federated poisoning risks."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: The Stuxnet worm modified industrial centrifuge control parameters while continuing to report normal operational telemetry to facility operators. Explain the direct architectural parallel between this attack mechanism and a data poisoning backdoor attack on an ML model.", "md": "In both cases, the compromise subverts operational integrity while silently evading standard health monitoring. Just as Stuxnet altered physical controller commands while spoofing healthy telemetry, a backdoored ML model maintains high validation accuracy on clean benchmark data while silently triggering malicious misclassifications only when an attacker's specific trigger pattern is present."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-13-03-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "In 2018, an aggregated activity heatmap revealed sensitive military locations through distinctive activity patterns in sparsely populated areas. What fundamental systems privacy vulnerability did this incident demonstrate?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The Strava incident showed that aggregation alone can expose sensitive locations when behavioral structure is distinctive. An individual-level DP guarantee would not automatically hide an institution or population-level activity pattern; its protected contribution and release objective must be stated. Even without user identifiers, unique coordinate patterns in low-density regions directly reveal sensitive operational locations. A database breach hypothesis is incorrect because no raw database or identity records were stolen; the visualization itself leaked the information. A power analysis hypothesis confuses physical side channels with macro-level telemetry visualization. A TLS certificate hypothesis confuses transport encryption with semantic information leakage in published aggregates.", "type": "single", "options": ["The platform suffered an unencrypted SQL database breach that leaked user account names and plaintext GPS coordinates.", "An adversary executed a physical side-channel power analysis attack against user GPS hardware chips.", "Aggregation can expose sensitive locations in sparse environments where high-dimensional behavioral traces retain distinct structural patterns.", "The web application failed to validate TLS 1.3 certificates on incoming client telemetry streams."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: analyze why simple aggregation fails to protect privacy in high-dimensional behavioral datasets."]}, {"q": "The 2015 remote hack of a Jeep Cherokee exploited a cellular connection in the vehicle's infotainment system to send unauthorized commands to the safety-critical CAN bus controlling brakes and steering. Which architectural safeguard directly addresses the equivalent vulnerability in autonomous ML robotic systems?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Segmentation and narrow authenticated gateways limit the authority an externally reachable component can exercise over actuators. Controllers must validate permitted commands and use a system-specific safe response to invalid inputs or communication failure. A model architecture change or DP noise does not establish that control boundary; the safe response depends on consequences, not an anomaly score alone.", "type": "single", "options": ["Upgrading perception models from convolutional neural networks to vision transformers.", "Applying differential privacy noise to perception model bounding box outputs.", "Storing model weights in unencrypted pickle files on edge microcontrollers.", "Enforcing strict hardware and network segmentation between untrusted inference interfaces and safety-critical actuator control planes, backed by fail-safe defaults."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the isolation architecture required between external-facing ML inference APIs and physical actuators."]}, {"q": "In edge-deployed ML fleets and federated learning systems, compromised IoT endpoints with default credentials represent only a bandwidth-exhaustion (DDoS) threat to serving infrastructure.", "explanation": "False. While compromised IoT endpoints can be marshaled for bandwidth DDoS attacks, in ML systems they also present severe algorithmic threats: they can inject poisoned gradient updates into federated aggregation pipelines, exfiltrate local biometric and sensor telemetry, and execute distributed model extraction queries while masking the attacker's identity.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the dual threat posed by compromised edge devices in federated ML architectures."]}, {"q": "Stuxnet chained four ____ exploits, vulnerabilities for which zero days of vendor patch or defense exist, demonstrating how unpatched dependencies in ML serving frameworks can expose air-gapped or production clusters to immediate compromise.", "explanation": "The correct answer is zero-day. A zero-day exploit targets a previously undisclosed vulnerability for which developers have had zero days to create and deploy patches. In ML systems, unpatched zero-day flaws in model-serving runtimes or serialization parsers create critical exposure windows before security updates can be distributed.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["The correct answer is zero day", "The correct answer is zero-day"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the vulnerability window represented by zero-day exploits in ML serving infrastructure."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze why simple aggregation fails to protect privacy in high-dimensional behavioral datasets.", "You can now compare the attack mechanism of the Stuxnet worm with model backdoor attacks in ML pipelines.", "You can now evaluate the isolation architecture required between external-facing ML inference APIs and physical actuators.", "You can now analyze the dual threat posed by compromised edge devices in federated ML architectures."]}], "id": "v2-13-03-learning-from-security-breaches", "chapter": "v2-13", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-13-04-systematic-threat-analysis-and-r": {"title": "Systematic Threat Analysis and Risk Assessment", "summary": "Classify ML security threats across a 2x2 likelihood-impact prioritization matrix to guide engineering resource allocation.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-13", "sections": ["Systematic Threat Analysis and Risk Assessment", "Security threat modeling for ML systems", "Threat prioritization framework"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Systematic Threat Analysis and Risk Assessment** in *Security & Privacy* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Classify ML security threats across a 2x2 likelihood-impact prioritization matrix to guide engineering resource allocation\n- Analyze the necessary structural components of an ML threat model to ensure actionable engineering controls\n- Evaluate threat vector scenarios across the access type, knowledge level, and lifecycle timing taxonomy\n- Design fail-safe default mechanisms for automated ML pipelines under active attack\n- Evaluate vulnerability persistence across the ML data pipeline lifecycle"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Security & Privacy, § Systematic Threat Analysis and Risk Assessment", "md": "An authenticated request can still probe a model for memorized data, and an accepted training sample can still carry a backdoor. Network security and user authentication therefore need a threat model that includes learned behavior. Training data can be manipulated to embed backdoors, input perturbations can exploit learned decision boundaries, and systematic API queries can expose enough behavior to train a substitute. Each attack uses an interface the system needs for ordinary work."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Security threat modeling for ML systems\n- Threat prioritization framework\n\nPay attention to: Threat prioritization matrix (Likelihood vs Impact); Four fields of an operational threat model (Asset, Boundary, Adversary, Control); Threat vector taxonomy (Access type, Knowledge level, Timing); Fail-safe defaults and minimal attack surface."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A security audit notes that an ML platform's risk assessment consists only of a qualitative list of potential failure modes without actionable boundaries. Name the four essential fields required for a complete, operational ML threat model and explain the role of the boundary field.", "md": "The four essential fields of an operational ML threat model are Asset (what must remain confidential, intact, or available), Boundary (where trust levels change), Adversary (permitted capabilities, knowledge, and goal), and Control (the permission, mechanism, or observable signal that constrains the attack or supports a response). The Boundary field defines the exact interface where trust transitions, such as between external user prompts and system instructions, raw training data and the model registry, or shared tenant processes on an accelerator, determining where enforcement must occur."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-13-04-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A public image-classification API admits untrusted annotations. Its threat assessment finds poisoning both likely and severely damaging, while accessible behavioral extraction has limited competitive impact in this deployment. How should the team prioritize these threats?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Under the supplied likelihood and impact assessments, poisoning warrants priority controls on data admission and provenance; query budgets and output restrictions are proportionate starting points for extraction. Their effectiveness still requires evaluation. These placements are deployment assumptions, not permanent ranks of attack families; sensitive data or greater copying harm could change them.", "type": "single", "options": ["Data poisoning is High-Likelihood / High-Impact requiring automated dataset validation; model extraction is High-Likelihood / Low-to-Moderate-Impact addressed via rate limiting and output truncation.", "Both threats are Low-Likelihood / Low-Impact and should be deferred until a physical hardware breach occurs.", "Model extraction is Low-Likelihood / High-Impact requiring full homomorphic encryption; data poisoning is High-Likelihood / Low-Impact requiring only API key rotation.", "Data poisoning is Low-Likelihood / High-Impact requiring physical air-gaps; model extraction is Low-Likelihood / Low-Impact requiring no action."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: classify ML security threats across a 2x2 likelihood-impact prioritization matrix to guide engineering resource allocation."]}, {"q": "An adversary queries an open prediction endpoint, records confidence scores, and trains a surrogate. The actor knows the task and output format but has no weights, source code, architecture details, or training records. How should access, auxiliary knowledge, and timing be classified?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Prediction queries provide black-box access, while the supplied task and interface knowledge must be recorded separately. Querying the live model is inference-time activity. Model visibility does not determine all auxiliary knowledge or grant write access; no installation or configuration change is described.", "type": "single", "options": ["White-box access, full knowledge, training-time attack.", "Black-box access, limited task/interface knowledge, inference-time attack.", "Gray-box access, full knowledge, deployment-time attack.", "Black-box access, limited task/interface knowledge, postdeployment configuration attack."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate threat vector scenarios across the access type, knowledge level, and lifecycle timing taxonomy."]}, {"q": "Compromising raw data during the data collection or labeling stage creates transient vulnerabilities that are automatically eradicated whenever the model is retrained on subsequent data iterations.", "explanation": "False. Poisoned samples, labels, or triggers can survive retraining if the next run reuses compromised data or an affected checkpoint. Retraining alone does not establish their removal. Provenance tracking and dataset auditing help locate compromised inputs; the team must also assess affected artifacts and verify the repaired training path.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate vulnerability persistence across the ML data pipeline lifecycle."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: An ML pipeline cannot establish a caller's authorization, while a separate behavioral monitor flags an unusual but potentially legitimate workload. Explain the fail-safe default for the first case and how to choose a response to the second.", "md": "A request whose authority cannot be established must receive no additional privileges; for example, deny an unauthenticated registry write. A behavioral anomaly instead calls for a response based on evidence and the consequences of continued operation and interruption. The policy may reject a request, suspend a release, use a verified fallback, or isolate a component while preserving essential service; the anomaly score alone cannot choose safely."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now classify ML security threats across a 2x2 likelihood-impact prioritization matrix to guide engineering resource allocation.", "You can now analyze the necessary structural components of an ML threat model to ensure actionable engineering controls.", "You can now evaluate threat vector scenarios across the access type, knowledge level, and lifecycle timing taxonomy.", "You can now design fail-safe default mechanisms for automated ML pipelines under active attack."]}], "id": "v2-13-04-systematic-threat-analysis-and-r", "chapter": "v2-13", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-13-05-model-specific-attack-vectors": {"title": "Model-Specific Attack Vectors", "summary": "Compare the attack surfaces and defensive mechanisms for exact artifact theft versus approximate behavioral theft.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-13", "sections": ["Model-Specific Attack Vectors", "Model theft", "Leakage through authorized outputs", "Data poisoning", "Adversarial attacks", "LLM-specific attack vectors", "Availability abuse and denial of wallet"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Model-Specific Attack Vectors** in *Security & Privacy* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Compare the attack surfaces and defensive mechanisms for exact artifact theft versus approximate behavioral theft\n- Analyze the nested objectives of data poisoning and their evaluation costs\n- Calculate extraction economics and evaluate output shaping controls on public ML APIs\n- Analyze why physical-world adversarial perturbations survive environmental sensing variations\n- Evaluate the stealth characteristics of backdoor poisoning attacks during standard model validation"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Security & Privacy, § Model-Specific Attack Vectors", "md": "A stop sign with strategically placed black and white stickers can remain recognizable to a human while an evaluated image classifier labels it as a speed limit sign. The attacker changes the observed input rather than the classifier's code or stored weights. This adversarial example illustrates why the lifecycle entry point matters. Training data, model artifacts, and runtime inputs give an attacker different ways to influence the system and require controls at different boundaries."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Model theft\n- Leakage through authorized outputs\n- Data poisoning\n- Adversarial attacks\n- LLM-specific attack vectors\n- Availability abuse and denial of wallet\n\nPay attention to: Exact artifact theft vs approximate behavioral theft; Data poisoning as bilevel optimization; Empirical model extraction costs and output shaping defenses; Physical-world adversarial perturbations as an inference-time threat."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Data poisoning is formalized as a bilevel optimization problem. State the attacker's untargeted outer objective and the defender's inner training objective, and explain why evaluating a poison's learning effect can be computationally demanding.", "md": "The untargeted outer objective chooses poisoning data to increase the trained model's loss on the target test set; the inner objective trains on the combined clean and poisoned data. Evaluating that learning effect may require repeated training runs, approximations, or surrogate feedback. The nested attack objective does not imply that every poisoning defense must simulate full training to convergence."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-13-05-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A security architect must deploy defenses against two distinct model theft vectors: (1) an unauthorized party copying serialized model checkpoints from a cloud bucket, and (2) a competitor sending millions of synthetic queries to a public REST endpoint to distill a student model. Which combination of defenses correctly targets each vector?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Exact artifact theft (Vector 1) is a storage/registry security failure constrained by encrypting artifacts at rest and enforcing strict IAM/RBAC access controls. Signing supplies artifact-authenticity evidence rather than preventing an observer from copying weights. Approximate behavioral theft (Vector 2) is an API interface leakage failure mitigated by rate limiting, monitoring for systematic query probing (e.g. KL divergence anomalies), truncating confidence scores, and returning only top-$k$ predictions. Applying output truncation to checkpoint theft misplaces API controls on storage assets. Air-gapping an inference server disables public API functionality rather than managing query information leakage. Using Python pickle serialization increases vulnerability by introducing arbitrary code execution risks.", "type": "single", "options": ["Vector (1) requires output rounding and top-$k$ truncation; Vector (2) requires hardware security modules for model weights at rest.", "Vector (1) requires differential privacy during training; Vector (2) requires air-gapping the inference server from the internet.", "Vector (1) requires encrypted storage, signed model artifacts, and strict IAM/RBAC permissions; Vector (2) requires rate limiting, query anomaly monitoring, and confidence-score truncation.", "Both vectors are mitigated exclusively by converting model checkpoints to Python pickle files."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: compare the attack surfaces and defensive mechanisms for exact artifact theft versus approximate behavioral theft."]}, {"q": "Research on language model APIs (e.g., Carlini et al., 2024) demonstrated that output-projection matrices of models like OpenAI `ada` and `babbage` could be extracted with root-mean-square errors around \\$10^{-4}$ using fewer than \\$4 \\times 10^6$ queries at a cost of under \\$15. In an image API serving a 1,000-class classifier, what is the systems effect of replacing full softmax probability distributions with top-5 class predictions and rounding confidences to 2 decimal places?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Returning five of 1,000 scores removes 99.5% of entries per response, and rounding reduces representational precision. Neither count establishes the extractor's query cost or unchanged application utility; measure both under the actual interface and attack. Output shaping alone supplies no DP guarantee and does not change stored model size.", "type": "single", "options": ["It completely eliminates all adversarial perturbation vectors while increasing inference latency by 100x.", "It introduces a formal $(\\epsilon, \\delta)$ differential privacy guarantee across all subsequent user queries.", "It reduces model parameter size in GPU VRAM by 99.5% during the forward pass.", "It returns 99.5% fewer distribution entries and coarser confidences; extraction resistance and legitimate-client utility require separate measurement."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: calculate extraction economics and evaluate output shaping controls on public ML APIs."]}, {"q": "A model compromised by a backdoor attack (such as BadNets) will exhibit a noticeable drop in validation accuracy on standard, clean test datasets, allowing automated validation pipelines to flag the compromise before deployment.", "explanation": "False. A backdoor can preserve clean validation accuracy while activating only on a particular trigger. Clean-set evaluation therefore cannot establish that a model is free of backdoors. Attack success and detectability depend on the trigger, data, model, and training procedure; targeted testing and provenance controls provide additional evidence.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the stealth characteristics of backdoor poisoning attacks during standard model validation."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: In the road-sign study, stickers on a stop sign changed only the input a deployed classifier received. Explain why a numerical perturbation norm alone does not establish physical feasibility or human recognizability, and what a physical threat model must state in addition.", "md": "A norm bounds distance in the chosen input representation; it does not specify printable placement, appearance, or survival through camera capture. A physical threat model must also state where the attacker can place a modification and which sensing conditions, such as distance, angle, and lighting, it must survive. Evidence gathered under one set of capture conditions is not a guarantee for every road condition or for the response of a complete autonomous vehicle."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: A team runs a shadow-model membership-inference audit against its model and finds attack accuracy barely above chance. Explain what this result establishes, what it does not, and what would supply a bound that holds against any membership test.", "md": "The audit shows that this attack, under its stated access assumptions, found little leakage, so it is a lower bound on what the model reveals: a stronger attack, different access such as released parameters, or auxiliary information could still succeed, and rare or outlier records may be exposed even when average accuracy is near chance. A bound against every test comes from training with an accounted differential privacy mechanism, because a membership test is a computation on the released model and the guarantee limits its verdict directly. The gap between the audit and the accounted bound is the uncertainty in the privacy claim."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now compare the attack surfaces and defensive mechanisms for exact artifact theft versus approximate behavioral theft.", "You can now analyze the nested objectives of data poisoning and their evaluation costs.", "You can now calculate extraction economics and evaluate output shaping controls on public ML APIs.", "You can now analyze why physical-world adversarial perturbations survive environmental sensing variations."]}], "id": "v2-13-05-model-specific-attack-vectors", "chapter": "v2-13", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-13-06-fleet-internal-trust-boundaries": {"title": "Fleet-Internal Trust Boundaries", "summary": "Quantify the communication cost that robust aggregation imposes on data-parallel training.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-13", "sections": ["Fleet-Internal Trust Boundaries", "Gradient synchronization integrity", "Collective traffic confidentiality", "Shared accelerators and caches", "Attestation and key custody at fleet scale"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Fleet-Internal Trust Boundaries** in *Security & Privacy* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Quantify the communication cost that robust aggregation imposes on data-parallel training\n- Evaluate how to measure the capacity cost of multi-tenant isolation controls under a matched resource budget\n- Identify the confidentiality assumption behind unencrypted collective communication\n- Analyze how shared serving state creates cross-tenant timing channels\n- Place fleet attestation and key custody costs on the coordination axis of the Fleet Law"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Security & Privacy, § Fleet-Internal Trust Boundaries", "md": "A training job on the reference cluster of … GPUs (… nodes with … GPUs each) depends on contributions from many participants. Each optimizer step of the Archetype A (GPT-4/Llama-3) reference model reduces … of FP16 gradients across the job, and each checkpoint interval produces a … artifact containing valuable learned state. Public inference controls do not govern these internal exchanges."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Gradient synchronization integrity\n- Collective traffic confidentiality\n- Shared accelerators and caches\n- Attestation and key custody at fleet scale\n\nPay attention to: Breakdown point of mean gradient aggregation; Traffic cost of robust aggregation vs. ring AllReduce; Confidentiality of unencrypted RDMA collective traffic; Residual state and prefix-cache timing channels on shared accelerators."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Two hypothetical serving configurations use the same allocated accelerator capacity, models, request mix, and latency target. The baseline sustains 1,000 tokens/s and the configuration with additional security controls sustains 850 tokens/s. Explain what this 15% capacity loss establishes and why comparing a whole GPU against a smaller MIG partition would not isolate security overhead.", "md": "The matched scenario requires capacity planning around a 15% throughput loss; it does not measure a universal isolation cost or identify a particular mechanism. A whole-GPU versus smaller-partition comparison also changes available compute and memory capacity. MIG partitioning can affect pooling and fragmentation, which must be distinguished from execution overhead; it does not inherently imply secure context switching. Measure the actual configuration under its stated security boundary."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-13-06-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A 64-worker data-parallel job replaces ring AllReduce with a gather-based coordinate-wise median implementation: every worker gathers every individual gradient before computing the median. Approximately how does per-worker gradient traffic change under this schedule?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The specified gather receives (N-1)M bytes per worker, compared with \\$2(N-1)M/N for ring AllReduce, giving N/2=32. Ring summation does not expose individual gradients. This is the cost of the stated gather schedule, not a lower bound for every robust aggregation protocol.", "type": "single", "options": ["It rises by about 32 times, because this implementation gathers the 63 peer gradients instead of moving about twice the gradient size in the ring.", "It is unchanged, because this median implementation uses only the sum returned by ring AllReduce.", "It roughly doubles, because gathering all individual gradients costs one extra ring pass.", "It falls, because the implementation discards outlier gradients before receiving them."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: quantify the communication cost that robust aggregation imposes on data-parallel training."]}, {"q": "RDMA movement between accelerator memories establishes gradient confidentiality even when the fabric payloads are plaintext.", "explanation": "False. RDMA specifies a data path, not confidentiality. Plaintext protection depends on access to links, switches, endpoints, and management. Facility isolation restricts access without removing that boundary; even line-rate encryption adds latency and may change critical-path overlap.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: identify the confidentiality assumption behind unencrypted collective communication."]}, {"q": "A multi-tenant LLM service shares one prefix cache across all tenants, and cache hits are distinguishable in time-to-first-token measurements. What can a probing tenant infer, and which control removes this cross-tenant cache-reuse path?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. A cache hit returns the first token measurably sooner than a miss, so probing can provide evidence of prior processing of that prefix. This does not identify a particular user or recover arbitrary prompts. Per-tenant cache scoping removes the cross-tenant reuse state at the price of lost reuse; other timing channels require separate assessment. Weight encryption at rest protects stored artifacts, not a timing difference at serving time. Mutual TLS authenticates peers but does not hide cache behavior. Output rounding limits extraction through probabilities and does not affect time to first token.", "type": "single", "options": ["Evidence that a probed prefix was recently processed outside the probing tenant; per-tenant cache scoping removes this shared-reuse path at the cost of a lower hit rate.", "The model weights of the serving replica; encrypting the weights at rest closes the channel.", "Another tenant's API key; mutual TLS between client and gateway closes the channel.", "The exact output tokens of other tenants; rounding output probabilities closes the channel."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze how shared serving state creates cross-tenant timing channels."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: When a 128-node training job waits for fresh remote attestation and key delivery before admission or recovery, explain why that delay enters the coordination term and recovery path of the Fleet Law. Distinguish it from steady-state protected-execution costs.", "md": "The job cannot resume until the required evidence is checked and keys are delivered, so this delay lies on the admission or recovery critical path and contributes to coordination cost. Which components are attested and when re-attestation is required depend on the platform and policy. This placement does not imply that protected execution, encrypted communication, or later key-management events have zero steady-state overhead; those costs must be measured separately."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now quantify the communication cost that robust aggregation imposes on data-parallel training.", "You can now evaluate how to measure the capacity cost of multi-tenant isolation controls under a matched resource budget.", "You can now identify the confidentiality assumption behind unencrypted collective communication.", "You can now analyze how shared serving state creates cross-tenant timing channels."]}], "id": "v2-13-06-fleet-internal-trust-boundaries", "chapter": "v2-13", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-13-07-hardware-level-security-vulnerab": {"title": "Hardware-Level Security Vulnerabilities", "summary": "Analyze the impact of speculative execution CPU vulnerabilities on multi-tenant ML infrastructure.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-13", "sections": ["Hardware-Level Security Vulnerabilities", "Hardware bugs", "Physical attacks", "Fault injection attacks", "Side-channel attacks", "Leaky interfaces", "Supply chain risks"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Hardware-Level Security Vulnerabilities** in *Security & Privacy* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze the impact of speculative execution CPU vulnerabilities on multi-tenant ML infrastructure\n- Explain how physical fault injection alters assembly-level neural network execution\n- Evaluate the vulnerability of edge ML accelerators to differential power analysis side-channel attacks\n- Evaluate the security risk of unsecured hardware debug interfaces on production edge ML devices\n- Classify vulnerability vectors across the sequential stages of the hardware supply chain lifecycle"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Security & Privacy, § Hardware-Level Security Vulnerabilities", "md": "When a serving team moves model weights into production, it often treats encryption, access control, and API policy as the security boundary. Hardware attacks break that assumption. A processor can leak across isolation domains, a field device can be opened, a bus or power rail can expose signals, and a counterfeit component can enter the system before deployment begins. Hardware therefore belongs inside the threat model, as the substrate that can invalidate software controls after those controls appear to pass."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Hardware bugs\n- Physical attacks\n- Fault injection attacks\n- Side-channel attacks\n- Leaky interfaces\n- Supply chain risks\n\nPay attention to: Speculative execution and processor isolation bugs (Meltdown/Spectre); Assembly-level fault injection mechanisms (ReLU branch suppression); Power analysis side channels on ML accelerators; Learned (deep-learning) side-channel analysis of power traces."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: In laser fault injection experiments on embedded neural network microcontrollers (e.g., Breier et al., 2018), researchers targeted the assembly instructions implementing the ReLU activation function. Explain the assembly-level mechanism of this attack and its effect on the model's output.", "md": "In the illustrated ReLU implementation, a laser-induced instruction skip targets `jmp end`, the jump that normally bypasses the zeroing block after the activation value has been retained. Skipping that jump lets execution enter the zeroing block, forcing the activation to zero and potentially corrupting downstream classification. The demonstrated mechanism is a control-flow fault, not extraction or modification of stored weights."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-13-07-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Vulnerabilities like Meltdown and Spectre exploit transient execution in modern processors. What microarchitectural mechanism can let an attacker infer protected data across an intended isolation boundary?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Transient execution can leave cache effects even when its architectural results are discarded. Spectre exploits prediction to induce a victim to access data along a transient path; Meltdown exploits a permission-enforcement weakness during transient execution. Their prerequisites differ, but both can turn residual microarchitectural state into an observation channel. The other choices concern memory corruption, numerical representation, or bus encryption rather than this transient-execution channel.", "type": "single", "options": ["Transient instructions can encode protected data in cache state that remains measurable after the processor discards their architectural effects.", "Speculative execution permanently overwrites physical DRAM cells with ciphertext keys without notifying the OS kernel.", "The CPU floating-point unit truncates FP32 tensor activations to INT8 during branch prediction.", "Speculative branch predictors disable hardware-level AES encryption engines on PCIe accelerator interconnects."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the impact of speculative execution CPU vulnerabilities on multi-tenant ML infrastructure."]}, {"q": "An attacker places an oscilloscope probe near the power rail of an edge ML microcontroller running a proprietary convolutional network. How can the attacker infer model architecture or recover layer weights without accessing the device's firmware or API?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Power observations can carry signals correlated with data and operations, providing evidence for architecture or parameter inference. Recovery still requires suitable measurements, implementation knowledge, and a reproducible learned or statistical relationship. Merely placing a probe does not establish extraction; packet injection and firmware replacement use different attack paths.", "type": "single", "options": ["By sending malformed TCP packets that trigger core dumps over the microcontroller's UART interface.", "By measuring instruction-dependent power consumption waveforms where current draw varies with weight values, multiplication operand hamming weights, and ReLU pre-activation signs.", "By overwriting the microcontroller's secure boot ROM keys using over-the-air firmware updates.", "By inducing thermal shutdown to force the microcontroller to reboot into an unauthenticated recovery bootloader."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the vulnerability of edge ML accelerators to differential power analysis side-channel attacks."]}, {"q": "A production edge device exposes an unauthenticated JTAG/SWD interface that permits reads of plaintext model weights in flash. Physical access to that interface can bypass host software permissions for those reads.", "explanation": "True. Under the stated read-access and plaintext assumptions, the debug path exposes weights independently of host permissions. A connector alone does not defeat correctly enforced debug locks or encryption whose keys remain unavailable.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": true, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the security risk of unsecured hardware debug interfaces on production edge ML devices."]}, {"q": "Traditional side-channel cryptanalysis required expert analysts to manually filter noise, align oscilloscope waveforms, and apply statistical correlation power analysis. What key capability do convolutional neural networks provide when applied to side-channel trace analysis?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. A CNN can learn features associated with intermediate computations from suitable labeled traces. Predictions then support candidate-key scoring across measurements; useful transfer must be evaluated. The classifier does not mathematically break AES without observations or remove the device's power consumption.", "type": "single", "options": ["They mathematically break 256-bit AES encryption keys in $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ time without requiring physical measurement traces.", "They replace hardware security modules with software-only virtual enclaves that simulate quantum annealing.", "They learn leakage features from labeled traces and predict secret-dependent intermediate values, which an attacker combines with known inputs to score candidate keys.", "They prevent the physical device from consuming electrical power during sensitive cryptographic operations."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: analyze how convolutional neural networks automate and scale side-channel cryptanalysis."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze the impact of speculative execution CPU vulnerabilities on multi-tenant ML infrastructure.", "You can now explain how physical fault injection alters assembly-level neural network execution.", "You can now evaluate the vulnerability of edge ML accelerators to differential power analysis side-channel attacks.", "You can now evaluate the security risk of unsecured hardware debug interfaces on production edge ML devices."]}], "id": "v2-13-07-hardware-level-security-vulnerab", "chapter": "v2-13", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-13-08-comprehensive-defense-architectu": {"title": "Comprehensive Defense Architectures", "summary": "Analyze hardware memory constraints in Trusted Execution Environments for ML model deployment.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-13", "sections": ["Comprehensive Defense Architectures", "Hardware security foundations", "Secure model deployment", "Runtime system monitoring", "Secure model design", "Privacy-preserving data techniques"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Comprehensive Defense Architectures** in *Security & Privacy* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze hardware memory constraints in Trusted Execution Environments for ML model deployment\n- Evaluate why federated learning alone is insufficient for privacy and justify the necessity of secure aggregation\n- Compare the security implications of model serialization formats in production ML pipelines\n- Compare the computational latency and trust assumptions of Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) versus Confidential Computing TEEs\n- Explain how model watermarking supplies bounded evidence for an intellectual property claim"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Security & Privacy, § Comprehensive Defense Architectures", "md": "An adversary who bypasses an API rate limit may still face authorization checks, restricted outputs, or an isolated execution environment. These controls protect different assets and constrain different actions. Secure ML systems use defense in depth to limit what follows from a failed mechanism. The benefit depends on whether the remaining controls retain their enforcement power; several checks that share the same compromised credential or runtime do not provide independent barriers."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Hardware security foundations\n- Secure model deployment\n- Runtime system monitoring\n- Secure model design\n- Privacy-preserving data techniques\n\nPay attention to: TEE memory constraints (SGX 128 MB EPC limit vs ResNet models); Gradient inversion attacks and secure aggregation in federated learning; Model packaging security (SafeTensors vs Python pickle RCE); The cost of trusted compute (FHE vs TEE vs AES latency)."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: In federated learning, raw data remains on client devices, but Deep Leakage from Gradients (Zhu et al., 2019) demonstrated that an adversary observing raw gradient updates can reconstruct original training images pixel-by-pixel. Explain how secure aggregation mitigates this attack and describe its operational boundary.", "md": "Secure aggregation hides individual updates from the server by revealing only an aggregate under the protocol's stated participation and collusion assumptions. This blocks direct access to a participant's raw gradient, but it does not prove that aggregates or the released model reveal no information. Differential privacy separately bounds an individual's contribution; access control and lifecycle governance protect other privacy boundaries."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-13-08-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A team wants to run deep learning inference inside an Intel SGX enclave with a 128 MiB Enclave Page Cache (EPC). A ResNet-50 model with FP32 weights occupies approximately 102.2 MB of memory before accounting for intermediate activation tensors and runtime buffers. What happens if the combined memory footprint of the model and activations exceeds the available resident EPC capacity on a configuration that supports protected paging during inference?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The EPC holds resident protected pages; it is not the enclave's entire virtual address space. On a platform that supports protected paging, a larger working set can trigger page transfers and cryptographic processing. The cost depends on access patterns, page traffic, and platform capacity, not a universal 100x penalty. Paging neither quantizes weights automatically nor reroutes execution to federated learning.", "type": "single", "options": ["Protected pages can be paged to untrusted memory with cryptographic protection, adding workload-dependent overhead that must be measured against the serving deadline.", "The SGX hardware automatically quantizes all FP32 weights to 2-bit integers to force the model into the EPC.", "The CPU halts immediately and burns an internal fuse, requiring physical motherboard replacement.", "The inference pipeline automatically falls back to unencrypted peer-to-peer federated learning across local nodes."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze hardware memory constraints in Trusted Execution Environments for ML model deployment."]}, {"q": "A production ML platform is replacing legacy PyTorch `.pth` checkpoint files with the `SafeTensors` format for its public model repository. What primary security vulnerability does this change eliminate?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. PyTorch's default `.pth` format uses Python `pickle`, which can execute arbitrary code during deserialization (`__reduce__` exploit), creating severe remote code execution risks when loading untrusted model checkpoints. `SafeTensors` stores only raw tensor buffers and header metadata, removing pickle-style executable object reconstruction from the format; it does not guarantee the loader implementation or downstream model is free of vulnerabilities. `SafeTensors` does not defend against API query extraction. It does not provide differential privacy guarantees. It is a file format and does not alter the hardware execution mechanism.", "type": "single", "options": ["It prevents black-box model extraction queries against the deployed HTTP API endpoint.", "It eliminates arbitrary code execution during model deserialization by storing only tensor data buffers without executable Python object serialization.", "It guarantees $(\\epsilon, \\delta)$ differential privacy for the training dataset encoded in the weights.", "It reduces inference latency by replacing floating-point operations with optical matrix multiplication."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: compare the security implications of model serialization formats in production ML pipelines."]}, {"q": "To support an ownership claim for a proprietary neural network, developers may embed a model ____, such as a secret trigger-set response pattern, and test its effect on normal utility and its survival under model modification.", "explanation": "The correct answer is watermark. A watermark can supply evidence of model provenance, but it does not automatically prove ownership or survive fine-tuning. Evaluate false attribution, normal-task utility, and resistance to removal under the claimed threat model.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["The correct answer is watermark"], "hints": ["Think about: explain how model watermarking supplies bounded evidence for an intellectual property claim."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: When designing privacy-preserving cloud inference for sensitive patient records, compare the systems trade-offs of Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) versus Confidential Computing with hardware TEEs (such as NVIDIA H100 Confidential Computing) across latency overhead and trust assumptions.", "md": "FHE computes on ciphertext under cryptographic security and key-custody assumptions; it does not establish correct execution or protect every authorized output. Its latency and memory overhead depend on the encrypted circuit, model, parameters, and implementation. TEEs instead execute protected plaintext and rely on hardware, firmware, trusted code, and attestation. Their overhead also requires measurement. Compare both against the same workload and latency target while stating their different trust boundaries."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze hardware memory constraints in Trusted Execution Environments for ML model deployment.", "You can now evaluate why federated learning alone is insufficient for privacy and justify the necessity of secure aggregation.", "You can now compare the security implications of model serialization formats in production ML pipelines.", "You can now compare the computational latency and trust assumptions of Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) versus Confidential Computing TEEs."]}], "id": "v2-13-08-comprehensive-defense-architectu", "chapter": "v2-13", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-13-09-differential-privacy": {"title": "Differential Privacy", "summary": "Apply differential privacy sensitivity principles to evaluate estimation stability across dataset sizes.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-13", "sections": ["Differential Privacy", "Mathematical foundations and privacy parameters", "Privacy budget composition", "Quantifying the privacy-utility trade-off", "Decision framework for differential privacy deployment trade-offs"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Differential Privacy** in *Security & Privacy* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Apply differential privacy sensitivity principles to evaluate estimation stability across dataset sizes\n- Calculate and compare noise calibration for the Laplace and Gaussian differential privacy mechanisms\n- Analyze the computational and algorithmic steps of DP-SGD and explain the post-processing property\n- Apply the 5-step differential privacy decision framework to evaluate deployment feasibility\n- Evaluate privacy budget accumulation across sequential model training operations"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Security & Privacy, § Differential Privacy", "md": "Suppose an adversary queries a medical diagnosis model using the exact attributes of a known patient and compares the answers with what a model trained without that patient would return. Encryption and access control cannot help here. The adversary is an authorized caller, and the query reaches only the trained model, never the stored records. A difference in one pair of predictions alone does not prove a privacy violation."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Mathematical foundations and privacy parameters\n- Privacy budget composition\n- Quantifying the privacy-utility trade-off\n- Decision framework for differential privacy deployment trade-offs\n\nPay attention to: Mathematical formulation of pure and approximate (epsilon, delta)-DP; Laplace vs Gaussian noise mechanisms and sensitivity calibration; DP-SGD operations (per-sample clipping, noise addition, post-processing); Decision framework criteria and measured privacy-utility feasibility."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Outline the three core per-step operations in a DP-SGD training iteration, and explain why querying a fixed, fully trained DP model in production does not consume additional privacy budget.", "md": "First check prospectively that the next step fits the accounted budget, then sample with the declared Poisson probability and clip each per-example gradient to norm $C$. Sum the clipped gradients (zero for an empty lot), add $\\mathcal{N}(0,\\sigma^2C^2I)$, and divide by the fixed public normalizer $B$, the expected lot size, rather than the realized random count. Apply the update and record the step using the same mechanism assumptions. Reusing the fixed released model without consulting protected training data is post-processing and adds no training privacy loss."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-13-09-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "For a fixed, public roster with bounded salaries and salary-replacement adjacency, adding Laplace noise calibrated to global sensitivity ($\\Delta f / \\epsilon$) to compute a differentially private sum introduces an expected absolute estimation error on the calculated sample mean that scales independently of the dataset size $n_{\\text{records}}$.", "explanation": "False. While the Laplace noise added to the aggregate sum depends only on the global sensitivity Delta f and the privacy budget epsilon (b = Delta f / epsilon), the expected absolute error on the sample mean is divided by the number of records (b / n_records). Thus, the mean-estimation error scales as \\$1/n_records, meaning large datasets dampen the added DP noise while small datasets suffer substantial instability.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: apply differential privacy sensitivity principles to evaluate estimation stability across dataset sizes."]}, {"q": "A data engineering team computes an aggregate query with global ell_1 sensitivity Delta_1 f = 2.0 and ell_2 sensitivity Delta_2 f = 2.0. For pure epsilon-DP with epsilon = 0.5, what is the scale parameter b of the required Laplace mechanism, and why is the Gaussian mechanism preferred in deep learning DP-SGD?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Laplace scale is b=Delta_1 f/epsilon=2/0.5=4. Gradient clipping supplies an ell_2 sensitivity bound suitable for Gaussian noise, with composition evaluated by a compatible accountant. For nonzero sensitivity, shifted Gaussian densities have unbounded likelihood ratios, so the standard mechanism uses an approximate DP bound. Unbounded support alone is not the reason: Laplace noise also has unbounded support. The tightness of an RDP bound depends on the mechanism and accountant settings.", "type": "single", "options": ["$b = 0.25$; Laplace is preferred in deep learning because it eliminates per-sample gradient clipping.", "$b = 1.0$; Gaussian noise is preferred because it guarantees $\\delta = 0$ for any arbitrary query.", "b = 4.0; Gaussian noise matches an ell_2 clipped-gradient mechanism and supports mechanism-specific composition accounting, such as Rényi DP.", "$b = 10.0$; Gaussian noise is preferred because it adds zero noise to non-zero gradients."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: calculate and compare noise calibration for the Laplace and Gaussian differential privacy mechanisms."]}, {"q": "A healthcare startup develops an ECG arrhythmia detector using a dataset of 5,000 cardiac patients. The model is safety-critical (where misclassifications cause clinical harm, requiring $<2\\%$ accuracy loss). When evaluating whether to train this model with DP-SGD under $\\epsilon \\le 3$, what does the DP decision framework indicate?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Small datasets can make the privacy–utility trade-off difficult, but no universal sample-count threshold proves failure. Evaluate the actual task, mechanism, privacy unit, accountant, calibration, and subgroup errors against the safety criteria. Federated learning, secure aggregation, and access control address different boundaries and do not themselves provide differential privacy. Raising epsilon drastically weakens protection rather than providing noise-free privacy.", "type": "single", "options": ["DP-SGD should be used immediately because datasets with 5,000 samples achieve near-perfect privacy-utility trade-offs at $\\epsilon < 1$.", "DP-SGD will accelerate training throughput by 5x by eliminating the need for gradient synchronization.", "The startup should increase epsilon to \\$10^6 to obtain pure privacy without noise.", "The team must measure private-model utility under the stated privacy budget and clinical acceptance criteria; dataset size alone does not establish feasibility, and FL with secure aggregation is not a substitute for a DP guarantee."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: apply the 5-step differential privacy decision framework to evaluate deployment feasibility."]}, {"q": "If an engineering team trains and deploys 12 successive monthly versions of a recommendation model on the same user dataset, with each training run satisfying $(1.0, 10^{-5})$-DP, the cumulative privacy guarantee for the underlying dataset remains $\\epsilon = 1.0$ at the end of the year.", "explanation": "False. Basic composition gives a valid $(12, 1.2 \\times 10^{-4})$-DP bound for these twelve releases. A compatible accountant may tighten it; per-run upper bounds do not establish the exact cumulative loss or a lower bound above 1. Rotating keys does not reset release accounting.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate privacy budget accumulation across sequential model training operations."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: Why is basic, advanced, or Rényi DP accounting not a universal ranking from weakest to strongest? Identify the inputs that an accountant must match when evaluating a DP-SGD run.", "md": "These analyses provide bounds under different assumptions; their names alone do not determine the tightest usable guarantee. Basic composition gives a general bound across releases. A mechanism-specific analysis can improve it when applicable. For DP-SGD, match the protected unit and adjacency, clipping and noise convention, sampling scheme and probability, step count, and target delta. Then compare valid bounds for that same mechanism and release history rather than choosing an accountant by a fixed ranking."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now apply differential privacy sensitivity principles to evaluate estimation stability across dataset sizes.", "You can now calculate and compare noise calibration for the Laplace and Gaussian differential privacy mechanisms.", "You can now analyze the computational and algorithmic steps of DP-SGD and explain the post-processing property.", "You can now apply the 5-step differential privacy decision framework to evaluate deployment feasibility."]}], "id": "v2-13-09-differential-privacy", "chapter": "v2-13", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-13-10-selecting-and-sequencing-defense": {"title": "Selecting and Sequencing Defenses", "summary": "Analyze the prerequisite dependency structure of the ML Security and Privacy Maturity Model.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-13", "sections": ["Selecting and Sequencing Defenses", "Defense selection by deployment context", "Security and privacy maturity model"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Selecting and Sequencing Defenses** in *Security & Privacy* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze the prerequisite dependency structure of the ML Security and Privacy Maturity Model\n- Compare domain-specific maturity model paths between healthcare ML and autonomous driving perception\n- Design verification controls for the Model Integrity Boundary in a continuous ML deployment pipeline\n- Apply the principle of least privilege to access boundary enforcement in ML systems"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Security & Privacy, § Selecting and Sequencing Defenses", "md": "Moving from mathematical guarantees to production controls requires more than setting an epsilon value. A common mistake is attempting to deploy every defense at once, including differential privacy, trusted execution environments, adversarial training, model watermarking, and red-team automation, while basic controls such as access boundaries and encrypted transport remain weak. Two decisions prevent that mistake. The first selects the defenses a deployment context requires; the second sequences them so that each control rests on the ones beneath it."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Defense selection by deployment context\n- Security and privacy maturity model\n\nPay attention to: Prerequisite dependency hierarchy of maturity layers; Domain-specific maturity model progression (Healthcare vs Autonomous Systems); Model Integrity Boundary controls (signing, SHA-256 hashes, release gates); Principle of least privilege in access configuration."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Compare how a healthcare diagnostic ML system and an autonomous vehicle perception system should prioritize their progression through the ML Security and Privacy Maturity Model based on their distinct threat models.", "md": "A healthcare diagnostic system prioritizes privacy accounting, sensitive-data isolation, and governance evidence early because patient-data exposure and permitted use constrain deployment. An autonomous system emphasizes authenticated startup and model artifacts, physical threat evaluation, and safe fallback because perception failures can affect physical safety. These are threat-model priorities rather than a universal order: secure boot does not prove physical tamper resistance, and neither domain can omit its other applicable control boundaries."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-13-10-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "An ML team wants to deploy automated red-teaming adversarial evaluations and differential privacy accounting on their newly deployed recommender service. However, their model registry permits unauthenticated write access and internal API calls lack TLS encryption. According to the ML Security and Privacy Maturity Model, why is this defensive plan structurally flawed?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Interface evaluations and release accounting do not protect an unauthenticated registry or plaintext payloads. A compromised runtime may undermine a privacy mechanism before release or expose raw data, but it does not retroactively invalidate a correctly produced DP artifact's guarantee. DP accounting does not inherently require an HSM; control selection follows the actual dependencies.", "type": "single", "options": ["The open registry permits artifact substitution and cleartext channels can expose payloads; those paths need controls even when a correctly released DP artifact retains its release-level guarantee.", "Differential privacy cannot be mathematically calculated unless the model registry uses hardware security modules.", "Adversarial red teaming is strictly illegal under GDPR without prior completion of ISO 27001 certification.", "Red-teaming algorithms require unencrypted network channels to simulate black-box query traffic."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the prerequisite dependency structure of the ML Security and Privacy Maturity Model."]}, {"q": "To enforce the Model Integrity Boundary in a continuous deployment ML pipeline, which set of controls connects the served checkpoint to the approved artifact, assuming the verification and enforcement boundary remains trusted?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The Model Integrity Boundary requires cryptographic signing of model artifacts, SHA-256 hash checks to verify that loaded weights match the validated build, and automated release gates that verify provenance before allowing traffic routing, along with tested rollback paths. Output perturbation addresses API extraction, not artifact integrity. Adding DP noise does not authenticate an artifact; its privacy and utility effects depend on a calibrated mechanism. Storing unauthenticated pickle files creates catastrophic supply chain and code-execution vulnerabilities.", "type": "single", "options": ["Applying randomized output perturbation at the web proxy layer without checking checkpoint signatures.", "Cryptographic signing of model packages, SHA-256 checksum verification against an authenticated registry manifest, and automated release gates with rollback paths.", "Adding Laplace differential privacy noise to the model weights prior to saving checkpoints.", "Storing model checkpoints in Python pickle format on public, unauthenticated object buckets."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: design verification controls for the Model Integrity Boundary in a continuous ML deployment pipeline."]}, {"q": "At the foundational Access and Configuration layer of the maturity model, enforcing the principle of ____ ensures that data engineers, ML researchers, and inference services possess only the minimum permissions required for their specific lifecycle tasks.", "explanation": "The correct answer is least privilege. The principle of least privilege (often implemented via Role-Based Access Control, or RBAC) restricts each actor's and service's access rights strictly to the resources necessary for their operational role, limiting the resources reachable through those permissions across data, training, and deployment infrastructure. It does not rule out attacks against the enforcement mechanism or misuse of legitimately granted access.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["The correct answer is least privilege"], "hints": ["Think about: apply the principle of least privilege to access boundary enforcement in ML systems."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze the prerequisite dependency structure of the ML Security and Privacy Maturity Model.", "You can now compare domain-specific maturity model paths between healthcare ML and autonomous driving perception.", "You can now design verification controls for the Model Integrity Boundary in a continuous ML deployment pipeline.", "You can now apply the principle of least privilege to access boundary enforcement in ML systems."]}], "id": "v2-13-10-selecting-and-sequencing-defense", "chapter": "v2-13", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-13-11-fallacies-and-pitfalls": {"title": "Fallacies and Pitfalls", "summary": "Evaluate the failure of security through obscurity in machine learning deployments under Kerckhoffs's principle.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-13", "sections": ["Fallacies and Pitfalls", "Summary", "Summary"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Fallacies and Pitfalls** in *Security & Privacy* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Evaluate the failure of security through obscurity in machine learning deployments under Kerckhoffs's principle\n- Analyze the high leverage of surgical data poisoning and backdoor attacks against large-scale training pipelines\n- Calculate and evaluate the attack surface expansion when scaling from single-node to distributed ML training\n- Analyze system-level vulnerabilities arising from uncoordinated component-level security controls\n- Evaluate the misconception that decentralization in federated learning guarantees privacy"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Security & Privacy, § Fallacies and Pitfalls", "md": "A common ML security failure is treating a local mechanism as a system guarantee. Obscurity, differential privacy libraries, federated learning, or encrypted storage each solve one part of the threat model, but none substitutes for lifecycle-wide reasoning about data, models, interfaces, hardware, and distributed scale."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Summary\n\nPay attention to: Failure of security through obscurity under Kerckhoffs's principle; Targeted poisoning harm versus aggregate validation accuracy; Distributed ML attack surface scaling (all-to-all O(N^2) channels); Isolated component security fallacy vs unencrypted logging bypasses."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A team assumes that because their training dataset contains 10 million images, an attacker would need to compromise at least 10% (1 million images) to cause meaningful damage. Explain why this assumption is a dangerous pitfall, distinguishing the attacker's targeted objective from aggregate validation accuracy.", "md": "The fraction of corrupted records alone does not determine harm. A targeted poisoning attack can influence selected predictions, and a backdoor can associate a rare trigger with an attacker-chosen outcome while preserving clean validation performance. Required poison counts and attack success depend on the data, model, training procedure, and access assumptions. Teams must test those mechanisms and trace data provenance rather than assume that a large clean corpus dilutes every attack."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-13-11-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A startup keeps its proprietary vision model architecture and hyperparameters secret, assuming this prevents adversarial attacks and model theft. Under Kerckhoffs's principle and empirical ML security findings, why does this defense fail?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Kerckhoffs's principle requires security to survive disclosure of system design apart from protected keys. Prediction APIs can support surrogate extraction, and perturbations can transfer between models under some threat models. Their success rates depend on the models, data, query access, and attack budget; no universal transfer percentage follows from secrecy. Attackers need neither the exact random seed nor a broadcast of the model weights.", "type": "single", "options": ["Adversarial attacks are physically impossible unless the attacker has the exact PyTorch random seed.", "Neural networks automatically publish their weights over DNS broadcast protocols upon initialization.", "Black-box queries can reveal model behavior, and adversarial examples can transfer between models; secrecy alone therefore does not establish a tested security boundary.", "Obscurity is valid for deep learning because gradient descent produces non-invertible mathematical functions."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the failure of security through obscurity in machine learning deployments under Kerckhoffs's principle."]}, {"q": "When scaling distributed training across 128 GPUs in 16 server nodes, an engineering team changes their communication topology from a logical ring to an all-to-all communication mesh. How does this architectural shift affect the system's communication attack surface?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. A logical ring has linearly many channels, while all-to-all has quadratically many. This count does not establish physical-link count, distinct credentials, or vulnerability count. Trace which actors can observe or modify each channel and how authentication and encryption protect it; the topology itself supplies no cryptographic guarantee.", "type": "single", "options": ["It reduces the attack surface from $\\mathcal{O}(n_{\\text{nodes}}^2)$ to $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ because all GPUs share a single global memory bus.", "It eliminates the need for node authentication since all-to-all topologies are cryptographically self-verifying.", "It maintains identical trust boundaries because gradient tensors are immune to network tampering.", "It increases logical inter-node channels from $\\mathcal{O}(n_{\\text{nodes}})$ to $\\mathcal{O}(n_{\\text{nodes}}^2)$; the threat model must inventory their endpoint, credential, and transport protections."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: calculate and evaluate the attack surface expansion when scaling from single-node to distributed ML training."]}, {"q": "Because federated learning keeps raw training data on client devices and transmits only model parameter updates, it is mathematically impossible for a curious server or network eavesdropper to extract private user training data without direct device compromise.", "explanation": "False. Exposed updates can support gradient inversion or membership inference under particular model, batch, processing, and observation conditions. Secure aggregation hides individual messages under its protocol assumptions; DP separately bounds a declared contribution in the release. Data locality alone supplies neither guarantee, and visible updates do not guarantee successful reconstruction in every setting.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the misconception that decentralization in federated learning guarantees privacy."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: An organization hardens its public inference endpoint with rate limits, top-$k$ truncation, and mTLS authentication, but leaves raw query-response logs in an unencrypted, world-readable monitoring bucket. Explain which API controls this observation path bypasses and what determines the information exposed.", "md": "A world-readable log bucket creates a separate observation path outside the API's request quotas and client authentication. What an adversary learns depends on the fields actually logged: raw prompts, embeddings, or pre-truncation scores can expose more than client responses. Those records may support surrogate training or sensitive inference, but neither reconstruction nor high-precision information follows from the bucket permissions alone."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now evaluate the failure of security through obscurity in machine learning deployments under Kerckhoffs's principle.", "You can now analyze the high leverage of surgical data poisoning and backdoor attacks against large-scale training pipelines.", "You can now calculate and evaluate the attack surface expansion when scaling from single-node to distributed ML training.", "You can now analyze system-level vulnerabilities arising from uncoordinated component-level security controls."]}], "id": "v2-13-11-fallacies-and-pitfalls", "chapter": "v2-13", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-13-99-test": {"title": "Test: Security & Privacy", "kind": "test", "summary": "Check what you learned across the whole chapter.", "blocks": [{"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-13-test", "pass_score": 0.7, "questions": [{"q": "A public image-classification API admits untrusted annotations. Its threat assessment finds poisoning both likely and severely damaging, while accessible behavioral extraction has limited competitive impact in this deployment. How should the team prioritize these threats?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Under the supplied likelihood and impact assessments, poisoning warrants priority controls on data admission and provenance; query budgets and output restrictions are proportionate starting points for extraction. Their effectiveness still requires evaluation. These placements are deployment assumptions, not permanent ranks of attack families; sensitive data or greater copying harm could change them.", "type": "single", "options": ["Data poisoning is High-Likelihood / High-Impact requiring automated dataset validation; model extraction is High-Likelihood / Low-to-Moderate-Impact addressed via rate limiting and output truncation.", "Both threats are Low-Likelihood / Low-Impact and should be deferred until a physical hardware breach occurs.", "Model extraction is Low-Likelihood / High-Impact requiring full homomorphic encryption; data poisoning is High-Likelihood / Low-Impact requiring only API key rotation.", "Data poisoning is Low-Likelihood / High-Impact requiring physical air-gaps; model extraction is Low-Likelihood / Low-Impact requiring no action."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: classify ML security threats across a 2x2 likelihood-impact prioritization matrix to guide engineering resource allocation."]}, {"q": "In 2018, an aggregated activity heatmap revealed sensitive military locations through distinctive activity patterns in sparsely populated areas. What fundamental systems privacy vulnerability did this incident demonstrate?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The Strava incident showed that aggregation alone can expose sensitive locations when behavioral structure is distinctive. An individual-level DP guarantee would not automatically hide an institution or population-level activity pattern; its protected contribution and release objective must be stated. Even without user identifiers, unique coordinate patterns in low-density regions directly reveal sensitive operational locations. A database breach hypothesis is incorrect because no raw database or identity records were stolen; the visualization itself leaked the information. A power analysis hypothesis confuses physical side channels with macro-level telemetry visualization. A TLS certificate hypothesis confuses transport encryption with semantic information leakage in published aggregates.", "type": "single", "options": ["The platform suffered an unencrypted SQL database breach that leaked user account names and plaintext GPS coordinates.", "An adversary executed a physical side-channel power analysis attack against user GPS hardware chips.", "Aggregation can expose sensitive locations in sparse environments where high-dimensional behavioral traces retain distinct structural patterns.", "The web application failed to validate TLS 1.3 certificates on incoming client telemetry streams."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: analyze why simple aggregation fails to protect privacy in high-dimensional behavioral datasets."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Poisoning as a bilevel attack**?", "options": ["De-identification and k-anonymity constrain only the shape of a released table, and federated learning, secure aggregation, homomorphic encryption, secure computation, and synthetic data each exclude a different observer, and none of them bounds what the released model or aggregate reveals.", "A poisoner needs write access to something the model learns from and solves a bilevel problem over the defender's training, so targeted, backdoor, and subpopulation attacks can leave aggregate validation accuracy unchanged, and clean-label poisons that keep every label correct pass any label audit, as concept poisoning of a text-to-image model (Nightshade) shows at a dose of a few hundred images.", "In synchronous data parallelism one compromised worker contributes to every replica's update and the mean's breakdown point is zero, so integrity needs robust aggregation rules whose gather-based traffic exceeds a ring AllReduce's.", "A model can be trusted only as far as a verifiable record of its data, code, weights, and hardware extends, so signatures establish origin but not benign content, and the release record must bind every input the artifact depends on."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Poisoning as a bilevel attack:** A poisoner needs write access to something the model learns from and solves a bilevel problem over the defender's training, so targeted, backdoor, and subpopulation attacks can leave aggregate validation accuracy unchanged, and clean-label poisons that keep every label correct pass any label audit, as concept poisoning of a text-to-image model (Nightshade) shows at a dose of a few hundred images.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Poisoning as a bilevel attack itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"q": "When scaling distributed training across 128 GPUs in 16 server nodes, an engineering team changes their communication topology from a logical ring to an all-to-all communication mesh. How does this architectural shift affect the system's communication attack surface?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. A logical ring has linearly many channels, while all-to-all has quadratically many. This count does not establish physical-link count, distinct credentials, or vulnerability count. Trace which actors can observe or modify each channel and how authentication and encryption protect it; the topology itself supplies no cryptographic guarantee.", "type": "single", "options": ["It reduces the attack surface from $\\mathcal{O}(n_{\\text{nodes}}^2)$ to $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ because all GPUs share a single global memory bus.", "It eliminates the need for node authentication since all-to-all topologies are cryptographically self-verifying.", "It maintains identical trust boundaries because gradient tensors are immune to network tampering.", "It increases logical inter-node channels from $\\mathcal{O}(n_{\\text{nodes}})$ to $\\mathcal{O}(n_{\\text{nodes}}^2)$; the threat model must inventory their endpoint, credential, and transport protections."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: calculate and evaluate the attack surface expansion when scaling from single-node to distributed ML training."]}, {"q": "An ML team wants to deploy automated red-teaming adversarial evaluations and differential privacy accounting on their newly deployed recommender service. However, their model registry permits unauthenticated write access and internal API calls lack TLS encryption. According to the ML Security and Privacy Maturity Model, why is this defensive plan structurally flawed?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Interface evaluations and release accounting do not protect an unauthenticated registry or plaintext payloads. A compromised runtime may undermine a privacy mechanism before release or expose raw data, but it does not retroactively invalidate a correctly produced DP artifact's guarantee. DP accounting does not inherently require an HSM; control selection follows the actual dependencies.", "type": "single", "options": ["The open registry permits artifact substitution and cleartext channels can expose payloads; those paths need controls even when a correctly released DP artifact retains its release-level guarantee.", "Differential privacy cannot be mathematically calculated unless the model registry uses hardware security modules.", "Adversarial red teaming is strictly illegal under GDPR without prior completion of ISO 27001 certification.", "Red-teaming algorithms require unencrypted network channels to simulate black-box query traffic."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the prerequisite dependency structure of the ML Security and Privacy Maturity Model."]}, {"q": "Compromising raw data during the data collection or labeling stage creates transient vulnerabilities that are automatically eradicated whenever the model is retrained on subsequent data iterations.", "explanation": "False. Poisoned samples, labels, or triggers can survive retraining if the next run reuses compromised data or an affected checkpoint. Retraining alone does not establish their removal. Provenance tracking and dataset auditing help locate compromised inputs; the team must also assess affected artifacts and verify the repaired training path.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate vulnerability persistence across the ML data pipeline lifecycle."]}, {"q": "A team wants to run deep learning inference inside an Intel SGX enclave with a 128 MiB Enclave Page Cache (EPC). A ResNet-50 model with FP32 weights occupies approximately 102.2 MB of memory before accounting for intermediate activation tensors and runtime buffers. What happens if the combined memory footprint of the model and activations exceeds the available resident EPC capacity on a configuration that supports protected paging during inference?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The EPC holds resident protected pages; it is not the enclave's entire virtual address space. On a platform that supports protected paging, a larger working set can trigger page transfers and cryptographic processing. The cost depends on access patterns, page traffic, and platform capacity, not a universal 100x penalty. Paging neither quantizes weights automatically nor reroutes execution to federated learning.", "type": "single", "options": ["Protected pages can be paged to untrusted memory with cryptographic protection, adding workload-dependent overhead that must be measured against the serving deadline.", "The SGX hardware automatically quantizes all FP32 weights to 2-bit integers to force the model into the EPC.", "The CPU halts immediately and burns an internal fuse, requiring physical motherboard replacement.", "The inference pipeline automatically falls back to unencrypted peer-to-peer federated learning across local nodes."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze hardware memory constraints in Trusted Execution Environments for ML model deployment."]}, {"q": "RDMA movement between accelerator memories establishes gradient confidentiality even when the fabric payloads are plaintext.", "explanation": "False. RDMA specifies a data path, not confidentiality. Plaintext protection depends on access to links, switches, endpoints, and management. Facility isolation restricts access without removing that boundary; even line-rate encryption adds latency and may change critical-path overlap.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: identify the confidentiality assumption behind unencrypted collective communication."]}, {"q": "To support an ownership claim for a proprietary neural network, developers may embed a model ____, such as a secret trigger-set response pattern, and test its effect on normal utility and its survival under model modification.", "explanation": "The correct answer is watermark. A watermark can supply evidence of model provenance, but it does not automatically prove ownership or survive fine-tuning. Evaluate false attribution, normal-task utility, and resistance to removal under the claimed threat model.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["The correct answer is watermark"], "hints": ["Think about: explain how model watermarking supplies bounded evidence for an intellectual property claim."]}, {"q": "Stuxnet chained four ____ exploits, vulnerabilities for which zero days of vendor patch or defense exist, demonstrating how unpatched dependencies in ML serving frameworks can expose air-gapped or production clusters to immediate compromise.", "explanation": "The correct answer is zero-day. A zero-day exploit targets a previously undisclosed vulnerability for which developers have had zero days to create and deploy patches. In ML systems, unpatched zero-day flaws in model-serving runtimes or serialization parsers create critical exposure windows before security updates can be distributed.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["The correct answer is zero day", "The correct answer is zero-day"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the vulnerability window represented by zero-day exploits in ML serving infrastructure."]}], "mode": "test"}], "id": "v2-13-99-test", "chapter": "v2-13", "minutes": 10}});
